<title>USA: U.S. Q4 labor costs rose 0.8 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q4 labor costs rose 0.8 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-01-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department employment cost data for U.S. workers in percent changes.</p>
<p>(Quarterly data seasonally adj.; Year-on-year data unadj.)</p>
<p> -			    Q4   Q3   1996  Q3 yr/yr  1995</p>
<p> Total Compensation    0.8   0.6   2.9    2.8     2.7</p>
<p> --Wages and Salaries  0.8   0.6   3.3    3.2     2.9</p>
<p> --Benefit Costs	 0.7   0.6   2.0    1.8     2.3</p>
<p> State/Local Govt	0.8   0.5   2.6    2.5     2.9</p>
<p> Private Industry	0.8   0.6   3.1    2.9     2.6</p>
<p> --Goods-Producing     0.7   0.7   2.8    2.8     2.4</p>
<p> --Service-Producing   0.7   0.7   3.2    2.9     2.8</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast a 0.9 percent increase in fourth quarter compensation for U.S. workers.</p>
<p>The Employment Cost Index on December 31 was 131.0 over its 1989 base of 100, compared with 130.0 on September 30.</p>
<p>Labor said that for private industry workers, wages and salaries rose 3.4 percent in the year ended December 1996, up from 2.8 percent in the year-earlier period.</p>
<p>The department said the increase in overall labor costs for 1996 was the largest since a 3.0 percent rise in 1994. The increase in wages and salaries in 1996 was the largest since a 3.6 percent increase in 1991.</p>
</text>
